# -*- mode: org; -*-

* 0.0.5-pre

** Delegation configuration

- Agent accepts a creation-time =model= override using an advertised
  =Backend:model= choice, applied after shared and per-agent preset hooks.
- Agent and Discussion accept =extra_tools= containing registered tool names
  or groups. Additions preserve default tools, confirmations and disabled
  tool-use settings; follow-ups retain the original configuration.
- Result-explorer uses the existing tool-policy system to enforce its scoped
  ResultRead/ResultGrep access even when extra tools are supplied.

** Compatibility fixes

- Grep and other agent tools can now return matches containing malformed
  UTF-8. Recoverable UTF-8 is decoded and unrepresentable characters appear
  as � in the text sent to the model. Saved tool results can be sent again.
  The introspector's =manual_names= and =manual_nodes= tools can also list
  entries containing malformed text. The Emacs Grep fallback accepts Unicode
  patterns and filenames.

- Resuming an Agent retains its exact tool-use mode and keeps disabled tools
  out of active request schemas; enabled tools still return after synthesis.

- File edits reject characters that cannot be saved in the file's fixed
  encoding before writing any part of a batch or recording its history.
  Automatic encoding selection for new and undecided files is preserved.

- Single-file patches now follow symlinks consistently with batch edits and
  review. Deletion preserves the link and removes its target, which history
  can restore.

- Skill discovery and interactive selections are local to each conversation.
  Requests refresh discovery when their directory or configuration changes,
  while in-flight requests retain their snapshots. Workers and discussions
  inherit discovery configuration and discover skills in their own directory.

- Saved sessions retain tool categories so same-named tools cannot be swapped
  on restore. Legacy snapshots with name-only references remain readable.

- Cloned chats receive independent MainChat identities, inboxes and request
  dispatchers even when session persistence is disabled. Closing a clone no
  longer stops the original chat's workers, discussions, shell jobs or timers.

- WebFetch treats brackets and braces in URLs literally, and hidden HTML
  elements no longer displace visible main content, articles or search results.

- Non-ASCII paths in rewritten tool calls no longer fail with
  ~wrong-type-argument json-value-p~ when an agent continues after approval.
  Embedded OpenAI tool arguments are decoded from UTF-8 before sending.

- Concurrent sub-agent tool approvals now have independent insertion markers
  and preview regions. Approving a worker no longer merges prompts with the
  main agent or disturbs its response text and prefixes. Task cards remain at
  the delegation point, before any unsent draft.

- Require gptel 0.9.9.6 or newer, matching the system-prompt and tool-steering
  APIs used by the package. Subagents now continue after rejecting a tool with
  steering instructions instead of stopping in the new steering state.

- Compaction with extra instructions now preserves gptel's parsed directive
  structure, including conversation templates and function-valued prompts.

- Markdown agent files without YAML frontmatter retain their prompt text.

** New features and UI changes

- Session titles now wait until at least one message has been sent. Autosaving
  an unfinished first prompt no longer names the chat. Title context excludes
  unsent follow-up drafts, and the submitted-message boundary survives restore.

- Saved sessions now generate a short conversation title on their first save
  with sent history, using the first and last messages (or the sole message).
  The title also renames the buffer to ~*gptel:TITLE*~ and remains unchanged
  through later saves and restores. Configure the title model with
  ~gptel-agent-session-title-preset~, using a named preset or plist just like
  ~gptel-agent-preset~. ~gptel-agent-session-generate-title~ refreshes a title
  on demand; ~gptel-agent-session-auto-title~ disables automatic generation.
  Browsing and restoring sessions never start title requests themselves.

- Web tools now use bounded asynchronous downloads and isolated extraction
  workers, preserving concurrent calls without parsing or laying out HTML in
  interactive Emacs. A shared FIFO queue, response size limits, cancellation,
  and deadlines cover searches, page fetches, and YouTube transcripts. Empty
  JavaScript pages suggest available browser tools, preferably delegated to a
  sub-agent that returns only relevant content and source URLs.

- Discussion reporting instructions now exclude notices about asking or waiting
  for user input, permission, tool approval or file review. These interactions
  use the child's local controls and echo-area notices without requesting an
  extra parent turn.

- Failed or cancelled request preparation no longer leaves discussion-enabled
  chats blocked from sending. Closing the last active discussion selected by a
  pending wait now releases the wait, and a busy parent chat points to ~gptel-abort~
  for recovery instead of the child-only steering command.

- Tool continuations now retain the assistant's narration before a tool call,
  including with streaming and immediate nonblocking acknowledgements. This
  repairs missing text in OpenAI, Responses and Anthropic request histories
  that could cause the model to repeat itself after calling a tool.

- Session saves now stage immutable chat versions and atomically publish a
  complete family manifest. Interrupted batches retain the previous complete
  checkpoint; new children join before publication, unchanged versions are
  reused, and missing members never trigger partial restore or automatic
  respawning. Existing flat archives migrate on their next family save.

- Session autosaving now stops its timer when the save queue is empty, avoids
  dirtying unchanged parents on child status events or empty inbox reads, and
  ignores unchanged setting assignments. Failed writes respect the save
  interval before retrying automatically.

- ~gptel-agent-sessions~ lists saved discussion families with chat counts,
  open buffers and save times. ~RET~ restores, ~d~ deletes a whole family
  after confirmation, and ~g~ refreshes. Deleting snapshots keeps live chats
  usable and excludes them and new descendants from autosaving. Use
  ~C-u M-x gptel-agent-session-save~ in a chat to save its family again.

- Opt-in ~gptel-agent-session-mode~ saves ordinary chats and their discussion
  relationships across Emacs restarts. ~gptel-agent-session-restore~ reopens a
  chosen family with transcripts, drafts, queued messages, supported settings
  and stable IDs; existing live buffers are reused. Restored delivery is paused,
  and temporary Agent workers or request callbacks are never replayed.
  Autosaving uses one dirty queue, bounded idle batches and an atomic file per
  chat, with a 30-second minimum interval per chat. ~gptel-agent-session-save~,
  buffer killing and normal shutdown save immediately. Private local storage
  is protected by a single writer lock.

- Invalid ~Glob~ and ~Grep~ arguments now return recoverable tool errors
  instead of terminating sub-agents. Their request-local schemas include the
  configured ~max_results~ and ~max_entries~ upper bounds.

- File tools now request ordinary tool approval for each operation outside the
  current project boundary, without changing the chat's working directory.
  Approval precedes instruction discovery and file review hooks and does not
  authorize later calls. The agent-controlled directory-changing tool is removed.

- Skills with ~disable-model-invocation: true~ are hidden from automatic
  discovery. In ~gptel-mode~, slash completion replaces ~gptel-agent-use-skill~.
  Accepting ~/skill~ inserts a plain Skill tool request displayed as ~/skill~;
  gptel sends the underlying text without a send-time expansion. The model uses
  the existing ~Skill~ tool for instructions and ~allowed-tools~, just as for
  other skills.

- ~Discussion~ accepts an optional ~model~ in gptel's ~Backend:model~ format.
  The tool advertises configured backend/model pairs and applies the selection
  after the preset and its hooks, overriding both backend and model. Omission
  preserves preset/inherited settings; unknown choices fail before creating
  a chat or worktree. Status results now include the backend and model.
  Sub-agent model configuration is unchanged.

- ~Glob~ and ~Grep~ now use asynchronous subprocesses with request-owned
  cancellation, a shared traversal/search deadline, and global entry, match,
  metadata and output limits. Grep advertises its native rg / grep -E / Emacs
  regexp dialect before use, and refreshes it only between model requests.
  Traversal prefers Python 3 and falls back to isolated batch Emacs. Glob keeps
  its basename matching, hidden/dependency entries, symlink handling and sorted
  output. Limited searches explicitly report incomplete coverage. Both tools
  accept ~max_results~ and ~max_entries~; Grep also accepts ~depth~. Local paths
  are required; blocking remote traversal is no longer attempted.

- ~DiscussionControl cleanup~ removes a finished discussion's generated
  worktree, keeping its transcript and branch. Optional ~delete_branch: true~
  also attempts safe Git branch deletion. Cleanup refuses unfinished work and
  never forces Git removal; status reports retain metadata and cleanup state,
  including when branch deletion must be retried after integration.

- ~Discussion~ accepts ~worktree: true~ to create a randomly named Git
  worktree and branch from the selected ~directory~, or the caller's directory.
  The child starts at the new worktree root. Creation and status results
  include the path, branch, source repository/directory and starting commit.
  Worktrees start at the source's committed HEAD and remain after closing.

- The bundled ~executor~ agent has been removed. The default agent now
  handles implementation and verification directly, with ~researcher~ and
  ~result-explorer~ available for delegated investigation.

- File review now offers ~A~ to ask about a proposal in an independent gptel
  aside, from the queue or either Ediff pane. It inherits conversation context
  and settings, includes the displayed revision and selected lines, and keeps
  questions and answers out of the original chat. Asking leaves the review
  decision unchanged. Follow-ups use ~C-c RET~; ~C-c C-r~ returns to Ediff.

- All gptel conversations now discover scoped ~AGENTS.md~ instructions, with private
  per-conversation registries and replaceable per-call instruction blocks.
  File mutations defer when their scope or instruction versions require a new
  model decision. ~gptel-agent-toggle-project-instructions~ controls the local
  preference; ~gptel-agent-describe-project-instructions~ shows discovered and
  delivered instructions. ~gptel-agent-use-project-instructions~ controls the
  global default regardless of preset, with per-buffer overrides.

- ~AskUserQuestion~ now queues questions with ~action: "add"~ (the default)
  and returns immediately. Repeated calls extend one live question buffer,
  preserving saved answers. Before waiting, the agent can revise questions
  by number with ~action: "update"~. Each changed question returns to
  unanswered and requires a fresh response. ~action: "wait"~ pauses the turn and
  collects all answers or cancellation. Users can save answers while the agent
  works and submit when it waits. ~gptel-agent-questions~ reopens the queue.
  If the model tries to finish with an uncollected batch, the runtime keeps the
  turn open and waits automatically, then resumes with the submitted answers
  or cancellation. Pending questions are no longer discarded on early completion.
  Question windows now open below their owning conversation, leaving unrelated
  windows alone. ~gptel-agent-questions-display~ can instead use ~same-window~
  to temporarily replace that conversation. Hidden conversations keep their
  questions queued until explicitly opened.

- ~Discussion~ creates independent, editable gptel conversations that survive
  their parent's turn. ~DiscussionControl~ monitors, steers, follows up, waits,
  answers questions and closes discussions. Tool approvals stay in the child
  buffer for the user to approve or reject; an echo-area notice identifies it.
  Replies and attention alerts stay in the child. The child uses
  ~DiscussionReport~ to send a chosen answer or useful update; only explicit
  reports wake idle parents or arrive at safe request boundaries. Parents can
  request reports with ~send~ or inspect work with ~read~. Unsent drafts defer
  automatic sending. ~gptel-agent-discussions~
  opens the conversation list; users can interact directly in each child chat.

- ~Agent~ accepts ~background: true~ for immediate launch acknowledgements.
  ~AgentControl~ lists, inspects, waits for, steers and cancels owned workers.
  Completion reports arrive at safe request boundaries. A request-local gate
  keeps parent turns open until all descendants finish and their outcomes are
  delivered, including when a model attempts an early final answer. Blocking
  calls and retained-conversation follow-ups remain supported.

- Oversized ~Bash~ output is now saved in full and returned as a bounded
  preview with an exact extraction query.  A new ~result-explorer~ agent
  can analyze only that registered result file through harness-scoped
  read/search tools and returns line-cited evidence for parent verification.

- Sub-agent time and model-round budgets are now configurable per agent with
  the ~task-timeout~ and ~max-request-rounds~ Markdown frontmatter or Org
  property keys.  Omitted limits inherit the corresponding global fallback
  options, which now default to nil.  The built-in researcher allows 3600
  seconds and 100 rounds, and result-explorer allows 1800 seconds and 60 rounds.
  The default web-tool budget is 50 calls per sub-agent run.

- gptel-agent now works over TRAMP.  This means it can run processes on
  remote servers, grep and edit files and so on without the need to
  install Emacs or an agent harness.  This involves calling command line
  programs on the remote, so it requires a POSIX-y remote environment.

- New user option ~gptel-agent-preset~ to specify additional gptel
  settings for sub-agents.  This is a preset that applies to all
  sub-agent calls, before any agent-specific settings.

  Its main purpose is to allow using a cheaper/smaller (or larger!)
  model for sub-agent tasks, which is a common need depending on the
  estimated difficulty of such tasks.  Of course, this is a gptel preset
  so you are not restricted to specifying a backend/model -- any gptel
  setting is supported.

  It can be set to a preset name (a symbol) or a plist spec, for example:
  #+begin_src emacs-lisp
  (gptel-make-preset 'lite              ;elsewhere
    :backend "Gemini"
    :model 'gemini-flash-lite-latest)

  ;; Apply the "lite" preset to sub-agent calls
  (setq gptel-agent-preset 'lite)
  #+end_src
  or just
  #+begin_src emacs-lisp
  (setq gptel-agent-preset
        '(:backend "Gemini" :model gemini-flash-lite-latest))
  #+end_src

- New command ~gptel-agent-compact~ to compact/summarize a conversation
  and free up the context window.  It works on the buffer up to the
  cursor position or on the active region, so you can compact a part of
  a conversation if desired.  Currently this must be invoked manually,
  but auto-compaction is planned for the future.

  Be warned: this is lossy and modifies the chat buffer!

  The prompt used for compaction is customizable, see
  ~gptel-agent-compact-prompt~.  You can also specify additional
  compaction instructions interactively by calling ~gptel-agent-compact~
  with a prefix argument.

  This command is experimental, and the implementation of prompt
  compaction might change in the future.

** Notable bug fixes

- ~Process~ returns invalid read/wait arguments and other operation errors to
  the model instead of aborting the request with a misleading HTTP 200 error.
  Tail-view guidance now explicitly says to omit ~offset~, including zero.

- Loading a skill preserves globally configured conversation tools across
  turns. Discussions inherit the parent's full active tool list, including
  tools activated by skills, without inheriting skill pre-approvals. An explicit
  preset can override the child's tools; ~DiscussionReport~ remains available.

- Discussion approval cleanup now supports gptel versions without the private
  overlay cleanup helper, preventing failed approvals from stranding worker
  tools. Worker ~TodoWrite~ calls use their own request and diagnostic buffer;
  completing a chat's todo list also clears its active-task header immediately.

- ~WebFetch~ now handles textual responses according to their content
  type instead of sending JSON, source files and plain text through the
  HTML readability parser.  HTML extraction falls back to a full-page
  render or the decoded response body, HTTP failures include status and
  response excerpts, and oversized output is bounded with an explicit
  truncation notice.  Requests remain noninteractive, and YouTube URLs
  are routed through the same tool to return descriptions and transcripts.

- Restored ~gptel-agent-preset~ application for sub-agent requests.  The
  option is read from the owning parent buffer and applied independently
  before per-agent settings, preserving named-preset parents,
  composition forms and pre/post hooks.  Unknown named presets now
  report an error instead of being silently ignored.

- Sub-agents now receive an explicit completion contract: negative and
  inconclusive findings are valid outcomes and must include attempts,
  limitations and confidence.  A repeated/cyclic call is blocked once
  to allow a strategy change; continued looping or an exhausted web
  budget now closes exploration and gives the same sub-agent a tool-free
  finalization turn.  The sub-agent retains its full transcript and must
  interpret the evidence and return its own final answer; raw tool output
  is not substituted for an agent response.

- The built-in researcher now prefers the current workspace for codebase
  tasks, treats sufficient successful results as a stop signal, and uses
  GitHub repository metadata instead of guessing branch names.

- ~WebSearch~ now defaults to 10 results, supports a configurable bound
  with ~gptel-agent-web-search-default-count~ and
  ~gptel-agent-web-search-max-count~, and returns query/count/coverage
  metadata.  Its instructions discourage identical retries and direct
  agents to fetch URLs derived from structured identifiers directly.

